# ---------------------------------------------------------------- secrets
# This repository is mirrored publicly. A key committed here is a key published,
# and git history keeps it after the file is deleted — so the rules below are
# broad on purpose, and `.env.example` is the one exception that has to be named.
#
# These are the first of three layers. The other two are `.githooks/pre-commit`
# (enable with `make hooks`) and `tests/test_no_secrets.py`, which fails the suite
# if a secret-shaped file is ever tracked. The test is the one that matters: hooks
# are not cloned and this file can be edited.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
secrets/
secrets.*
*_secret*
# ...but not the test that hunts for them. `*_secret*` matched
# `tests/test_no_secrets.py` by its name and quietly kept the only load-bearing
# layer out of the repository entirely: it ran here and existed nowhere else, so
# CI never ran it and no clone had it. `tests/test_packaging.py` now fails if any
# file under `tests/` is ignored, because a test git ignores is a test that does
# not exist for anybody else.
!tests/test_no_secrets.py
*credential*
*apikey*
*api_key*
*API_KEY*
openApikey*
.openai
